<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 300 · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="grc">Φῶς Ἱλαρόν</bdi> / <bdi lang="grc-Latn">Fōs Ilaron</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>One of the oldest Christian hymns still sung, in Greek, when the lamps were lit at sunset.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; attributed by some to Athenogenes of Pedachtoë or Basil of Caesarea, and revised by Sophronius of Jerusalem</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>early Christians of the Greek-speaking East; later the Orthodox, Eastern Catholic, Armenian, Anglican and Lutheran churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Koine Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first recorded in the Apostolic Constitutions, late 3rd or early 4th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous Greek hymn, recorded by the late 3rd or early 4th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron</a> is an ancient Christian hymn written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>, known in the Western Church by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title Lumen Hilare and in English as &quot;O Gladsome Light&quot;. It is one of the earliest known Christian hymns recorded outside the Bible that is still in use today. Its author is unknown. It was first written down in the Apostolic Constitutions, a work of the late 3rd or early 4th century, in a collection of songs for the morning, the evening, before meals and at the lighting of candles. It was to be sung when the lamps were lit in the evening, which gave it a second name, the &quot;Lamplighting Hymn&quot;. Unlike the other songs of that collection, whose words partly come from Scripture, it is considered the first hymn in the modern sense: rhythmic in structure, in twelve verses of five to eleven syllables.</p><p>By the time of Basil of Caesarea (329–379) it was already old: he spoke of its singing as a cherished tradition of the church, though some attribute the hymn to Basil himself. Others credit Athenogenes of Pedachtoë, a saint whose day is 16 July and who, by the account in the Roman Martyrology, sang a hymn of joy on his way to martyrdom by fire in Pontus and left it in writing to his disciples. His story closely resembles that of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> martyred with ten disciples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Sebaste</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, most probably about 305. Sophronius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> (560–638), a poet, is believed to have revised the hymn, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> liturgical books often name him as its author. A tradition links the hymn's early use to Jerusalem, where a lamp was kept burning in the empty tomb of Christ and a candle lit from it was carried out while the hymn was sung.</p><p>The hymn is a fixed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> vespers, sung or recited every day after the lamp-lighting psalms. The original melody used by the Greek Orthodox Church spans almost two octaves and is considered taxing on the voice. In the Armenian Rite, where it is called Luys Zvart, it is sung only on Saturday evening and on the eve of certain feasts.</p><p>In 1834 John Keble, one of the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, translated it into English as &quot;Hail, Gladdening Light&quot;, keeping the irregular metre of the original. No existing hymn tune fitted it, so John Stainer, organist of St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, wrote the tune <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Sebaste</a> for it, published in Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1875; Charles Wood set Keble's version as an anthem for eight voices in 1912. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a> and Robert Bridges made further translations. The traditional Anglican Evening Prayer did not call for it, but the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> of 1979 prescribes it as an optional canticle before the psalms, and the Lutheran Service Book of the Lutheran Church – Missouri Synod includes it in early evening prayer. Composers who have set it include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> and Rachmaninoff, in their All-Night Vigils, and Arthur Sullivan.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An evening hymn for the moment the lamps are lit at sunset. It praises Christ as light and, as the sun goes down, gives praise to the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. The mood is calm and joyful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Koine Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Koine Greek</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Φῶς ἱλαρὸν ἁγίας δόξης ἀθανάτου Πατρός,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Phōs hilaron hagias doxēs athanatou Patros,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Joyful light of the holy glory of the immortal Father,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">—οὐρανίου, ἁγίου, μάκαρος, Ἰησοῦ Χριστέ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">—ouraniou, hagiou, makaros, Iēsou Christe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>—heavenly, holy, blessed—O Jesus Christ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ἐλθόντες ἐπὶ τὴν ἡλίου δύσιν, ἰδόντες φῶς ἑσπερινόν,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">elthontes epi tēn hēliou dysin, idontes phōs hesperinon,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>having come to the setting of the sun, having seen the evening light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">—ὑμνοῦμεν Πατέρα, Υἱόν, καὶ ἅγιον Πνεῦμα, Θεόν.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">—hymnoumen Patera, Huion, kai hagion Pneuma, Theon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>—we hymn the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, God.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Ἄξιόν σε ἐν πᾶσι καιροῖς ὑμνεῖσθαι φωναῖς αἰσίαις,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Axion se en pasi kairois hymneisthai phōnais aisiais,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is fitting that you be hymned at all times with auspicious voices,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">—Υἱὲ Θεοῦ, ζωὴν ὁ διδούς· διὸ ὁ κόσμος σὲ δοξάζει.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">—Huie Theou, zōēn ho didous; dio ho kosmos se doxazei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>—Son of God, you who give life; therefore the world glorifies you.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous Greek hymn; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phos_Hilaron" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Classical transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'hilaron' is 'cheerful, gladdening'.</li><li>line 1.2: 'heavenly, holy, blessed' are genitive and agree with 'the Father' of line 1; 'Jesus Christ' is addressed.</li><li>line 1.5: 'aisiais', 'auspicious, fitting'; some texts read 'hosiais', 'holy'.</li><li>Accents and breathings are not shown except the rough breathing (h); the diphthong υι is written ui (Huion, Huie).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
